Teensy 4.1
==========

An NXP i.MX RT1062: a Cortex-M7 at 600MHz with eight megabytes of serial
NOR flash and a megabyte of RAM.

What is proved
--------------

The core.  os/inferno/armv7m is shared with os/inferno/mps2, which QEMU
models as an AN500 MPS2 and which boots to an interactive Inferno shell:
the vector table, the exception path, interrupt masking on PRIMASK, the
exclusive monitor, coherence, SysTick, and the copy of the data segment
out of the image into where it was linked all run there.  The whole of
libinterp runs there.  So does the floating point this tree's Thumb
linker encodes as VFP; appl/cmd/fptest.b is what says so.

The image is checked as far as it can be without a board: the FlexSPI
configuration block, the image vector table and the boot data are at the
offsets chapter 9 of the i.MX RT1060 reference manual gives them, hold
the values that manual gives for a serial NOR part in quad mode, and the
first two words of the kernel are a stack pointer in OCRAM2 and a reset
address in flash with its Thumb bit set.

What is not
-----------

Everything that is only this board's, because there is no emulator for
this part and this was written without one of the boards:

	the ARM PLL taken to 600MHz and halved
	the FlexRAM given over entirely to DTCM
	the watchdogs stopped
	the pin multiplexing for LPUART6 on pins 0 and 1
	the LPUART divisor for 115200 out of an 80MHz clock

The first things to check on a real board are, in order: whether the ROM
accepts the FlexSPI configuration block at all, whether anything comes
out of pin 1 at 115200, and whether the clock the LPUART divides really
is the 80MHz this assumes.

Running it
----------

	teensy_loader_cli --mcu=TEENSY41 -w iteensy41.hex

teensy41.bin is the same image as a plain binary.  Console is pin 1
(transmit) and pin 0 (receive) at 115200, which the board calls Serial1.

What is missing
---------------

USB, the SD card, ethernet and the display.  There is no ip stack, no
storage and no draw in the configuration because there are no drivers
for them.
